{#
  What an account would be for.

  A working page rather than a live one: nothing here is for sale, and the
  notice at the top says so in the first thing anybody reads. It exists to
  settle what falls on each side of the line before there is any machinery to
  enforce it — which is the order that matters, because a tier list written
  after the payment code is a rationalisation of whatever was easy to gate.

  Deliberately unlinked and unindexed. A pricing page for something that cannot
  be bought is a broken promise if a reader finds it by accident, so it is
  reachable only by its URL until there is something behind it. See
  `docs/DEFERRED.md`, "Accounts, and what a paid one is for", for the reasoning
  this page renders.
#}
{% macro tick() %}
  <svg class="plan__tick" viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
    <path d="M3 8.5 6.5 12 13 4.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
          stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
  </svg>
{% endmacro %}

{#
  The page is as wide as the cards need; everything that is prose keeps the
  measure and centres inside it. Two tiers side by side is the whole point of
  the layout, and the measure is 38rem — one card's worth.
#}
<div class="accounts">
  <div class="accounts__column">
    <header class="page-header">
    <h1 class="page-title">Accounts</h1>
    <p class="page-subhead">What is free, what an account would add, and what it would never touch.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="prose">
    <p class="notice">
      <strong>Nothing here is for sale.</strong> There are no accounts on this
      site and no way to pay for one. This page is where the line between free
      and paid is being worked out, written down before there is any machinery
      to enforce it.
    </p>

    <p>
      The rules are never behind a login. Every rulebook, summary, glossary and
      index on this site is readable by anybody, with no account, and that does
      not change. What an account would sell is durability and portability —
      your bookmarks surviving a cleared cache and following you to the phone
      you have at the table — and nothing else.
    </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <ul class="plan-grid">
    <li class="plan">
      <div class="plan__head">
        <h2 class="plan__name">Free</h2>
        <p class="plan__price"><span class="plan__amount">$0</span></p>
        <p class="plan__line">No account, no card, no expiry. The whole site.</p>
      </div>

      <ul class="plan__list">
        <li>{{ tick() }}<span>Every page of every game — rulebooks, summaries, glossaries, indexes, expansions</span></li>
        <li>{{ tick() }}<span>Search one game or all of them, and jump to any section, term or rule</span></li>
        <li>{{ tick() }}<span>Bookmark any section; My Reference gathers them onto one page in rules order</span></li>
        <li>{{ tick() }}<span>Favourite the games you play, and pick up where you left off</span></li>
        <li>{{ tick() }}<span>Reading settings — theme, text size, typeface, line spacing, Just read</span></li>
        <li>{{ tick() }}<span>Print any page, or your own reference, laid out for paper</span></li>
        <li>{{ tick() }}<span>Download a copy of everything you have made, and restore it from that file</span></li>
      </ul>

      <p class="plan__note">
        Kept in this browser only. Nothing is uploaded and nobody else sees it —
        which also means it goes when you clear your browsing data, and Safari
        deletes it after about a week away.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li class="plan plan--paid">
      <div class="plan__head">
        <h2 class="plan__name">Account</h2>
        <p class="plan__price">
          <span class="plan__amount">{{ plan.amount }}</span>
          <span class="plan__period">{{ plan.period }}</span>
        </p>
        <p class="plan__line">{{ plan.alternative }}. The paragraph above, fixed.</p>
      </div>

      <p class="plan__inherits">Everything in Free, and:</p>

      <ul class="plan__list">
        <li>{{ tick() }}<span>Bookmarks, favourites and history on every device you read on</span></li>
        <li>{{ tick() }}<span>Nothing lost when you clear your browsing data, or when Safari clears it for you</span></li>
        <li>{{ tick() }}<span>Learn a game on a laptop, look it up on the phone in your hand at the table</span></li>
        <li>{{ tick() }}<span>Your reference back after a new phone, a reinstall, or a browser you have never used before</span></li>
      </ul>

      <p class="plan__note">
        One tier. There is no version of this site with more rules in it.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="accounts__column">
    <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="promises">What an account will never do</h2>

    <p>
      These are the reasons the tier above is so short, and they are worth more
      than any feature that could be added to it.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>It will never hold the rules.</strong> Nothing anybody needs in
        order to read a game is behind it. A gate on something the site needs
        in order to be worth using would be a different product.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It will never hold your data hostage.</strong> Stop paying and
        you keep every bookmark, favourite and reference you made. The syncing
        stops; the data does not, and the export button keeps working whether
        or not you have ever paid.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>It will never be the only way out.</strong> Download a copy and
        restore from a file are free, now and permanently, so leaving is always
        a file rather than a negotiation.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="later">Not built, and not promised</h2>

    <p>
      Two things have been named as candidates for the paid side later —
      highlighting passages, and putting My Reference in an order of your own.
      Neither exists, neither is scheduled, and if either ever ships it will be
      additive to a reading experience that is complete without it. They are
      listed here so this page stays honest about what it is: the current
      answer to a question, not a roadmap.
    </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
